  line((0, -0.2725), (0, -0.9661), mark: (end: ">"))
  content((-0.2741, -0.6193), [#text(size: 0.73em)[$pi$]])
})

where $S \, Y$ are smooth projective varieties, $pi$ is birational, $f$ is surjective, and

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5)")
 $ d pi^(*) P - f^(*) A in overline("Eff") \( S \) $ <eq:domination>]

for an integer $d > 0$ and an ample line bundle $A$ on $Y$. The map to a point is included, with the polarization omitted. Choose such a diagram with $dim Y$ maximal.

We can require connected general fibers. Passing to the relative algebraic closure of $bb(C) \( Y \)$ in $bb(C) \( W \)$ gives a generically finite replacement of the base. The pullback of $A$ there is big. On a smooth projective model it dominates a small ample class in pseudoeffective order, so after multiplying (5) its required form persists. The same reasoning shows that smooth birational modifications of the base are harmless. In particular $bb(C) \( Y \)$ is algebraically closed in $bb(C) \( W \)$ for the resulting fibration. The finite map in the Stein factorization of the proper morphism $f$ is then birational. Since $Y$ is normal, that map is an isomorphism. Thus every fiber of $f$ is connected and $f_(*) cal(O)_S = cal(O)_Y$, as required by the direct-image theorem.

There is also a normal equidimensional intermediate model

